## Workflow
1. Start with cached startup state for "here/current" and inspection requests.
2. Use cached pane/surface/title/content data first. Only run cmux discovery commands when explicitly requested (refresh/current/live), or when the cache is insufficient.
3. Copy refs from current cmux output when a fresh call is required. Prefer refs over UUIDs unless the user asks for stable UUIDs or --id-format uuids/both.
4. For layout changes, target panes. For read/type/key actions, target surfaces.
5. Before sending text or keys, read the target screen unless the user explicitly says to send to the current surface now.
6. After every mutation, verify with cmux tree, cmux identify, cmux read-screen, or the narrow cmux command that proves the action.
7. If syntax is uncertain, run cmux --help or the narrow cmux docs command, then retry once with copied syntax.
8. For "focus the pane for FOO" or title-based matching requests, first resolve from cached titles/snippets in startup state, then run exactly one command if there is one best match: cmux focus-pane --pane PANE_REF. Do not run find-window/read-screen/tree for extra confidence.
9. For "rename all the titles" requests, resolve all matching surfaces from cache first, then apply cmux rename-tab per matched SURFACE_REF and verify with cmux tree.

## Mutation policy
Read-only cmux inspection is always allowed when it matches the task: identify, tree, list-*, find-window, read-screen, docs/help, top/memory, logs/status reads, browser snapshots, and config checks.

Live layout mutations are allowed only when the action and target are explicit or can be safely resolved from current cmux evidence. This includes creating/selecting/renaming workspaces, panes, splits, surfaces, tabs, focus changes, moves, swaps, resizes, markdown/diff/sidebar views, browser navigation, notifications, status/progress/log updates, ssh/vm actions, and raw RPC.

Closing a workspace, pane, or surface requires an explicit close request and a target identified by ref from cmux output. Never close guessed targets.

Sending terminal input, keys, ctrl+c, browser clicks/fills, SSH/tmux/VM commands, or raw RPC changes live state. Use the latest cmux evidence, target the correct surface/ref, and verify afterward. Never send destructive shell commands such as rm, kill, shutdown, or force commands into a surface unless the user explicitly asked for that exact command.

Do not edit project files or persistent cmux extension/config files unless the user explicitly asks. Persistent cmux customization belongs in imp-cmux-extensions.

### Sending commands to terminals
1. cmux send types text into the surface. The text only EXECUTES if it ends with \\n (a literal backslash-n inside the quoted argument). Without \\n the text just sits at the prompt.
   Correct: cmux send --surface surface:3 "npm test\\n"
   Wrong: cmux send --surface surface:3 "npm test"
2. To stop a running process: cmux send-key --surface SURFACE_REF ctrl+c
3. After sending a command, wait, then verify: sleep 2 && cmux read-screen --surface SURFACE_REF --lines 30
4. Never send to a surface that is busy running something else unless the user asked; check with read-screen first.

